comments.content:
TOCTOU between the allow-list check and the side-effecting call: `path_openable` (which canonicalizes) runs, then `Path::new(&path).exists()` is re-checked on the raw path, and finally `opener.open_path(path, …)` is invoked on the still-raw `path`. An attacker who can race a symlink swap in this window (e.g. bind a benign file, then between check and open replace it with a symlink to `.app`/`.command`) bypasses the boundary while keeping the same raw string. Mitigate by re-canonicalizing right before the open/delete and asserting the canonical form is still inside the allow-list (or by passing the canonicalized path to opener/trash).

```rust
    if !std::path::Path::new(&path).exists() {
        crate::bot::audit_log(
            &app,
            &format!(
                "open_file_path missing | {}",
                crate::audit::escape_for_log(&path, 200)
            ),
        );
        return Err(CommandError::IoError(format!(
            "路径不存在（可能已被移动或删除）：{path}"
        )));
    }
    use tauri_plugin_opener::OpenerExt;
    app.opener()
        .open_path(path, None::<&str>)
        .map_err(|e| CommandError::IoError(format!("打开路径失败：{e}")))
```

