comments.content:
Mixed normalization semantics between the two branches create observable, platform-dependent divergence: the bound-set branch uses raw-string `set.contains(path)`, while the gen_dir fallback canonicalizes both sides. Path forms that resolve to the same file (`./`, trailing slash, double slash, macOS `/private/var` vs `/var`, Windows 8.3 short names, symlinks) hit the bound branch with one string and the canonical branch with another, so the allow/deny outcome depends on which alias was used. Either canonicalize both sides uniformly (canonicalize the bound set once on collection, then string-match against canonical form), or document the raw-string contract and reject any bound path whose canonical form differs from its stored form.

```rust
fn path_openable_in(
    path: &str,
    set: &std::collections::HashSet<String>,
    gen_dir: Option<&std::path::Path>,
) -> bool {
    if set.contains(path) {
        return true;
    }
    let Some(gen) = gen_dir.and_then(|d| std::fs::canonicalize(d).ok()) else {
        return false;
    };
    match std::fs::canonicalize(path) {
        Ok(c) => c.starts_with(&gen),
        Err(_) => false,
    }
}
```

