Slicing `s.id` at byte offset 8 panics when the byte at that offset is not a char boundary (i.e. when `s.id` contains non-ASCII / multi-byte UTF-8 before byte 8). `SessionRow.id` is an unconstrained `String`, so any caller feeding in a non-UUID/non-ASCII id will crash the eval runner with `byte index 8 is not a char boundary`. Validate ASCII (e.g. `s.id.is_ascii()` → fallback to full id, or hash `s.id` and use hex) before slicing.

```rust
let prefix_len = 8.min(s.id.len());
// 必须落在 char 边界；非 ASCII 时退回全量
let prefix = if s.id.is_char_boundary(prefix_len) {
    &s.id[..prefix_len]
} else {
    &s.id[..]
};
```

`sample_sessions` silently assumes the input slice is already sorted by `updated_at DESC` and just does `.take(n)`. If a caller passes unordered rows the function returns a "most recent N" that is actually arbitrary — and the only test passes coincidentally because its fixtures happen to be in order. Either sort defensively here (single sort cost is trivial for ≤500 rows) or assert / panic with a clear message when the order invariant is violated.

```rust
// 防御性排序或前置断言：
