`Connection::peer_addr` requires `&mut self`, but the underlying `TcpStream::peer_addr` only needs `&self`. Forcing mutability here is unnecessarily restrictive: callers that hold a shared/`&mut`-borrowed `Connection` (e.g. for concurrent reads/writes) cannot call `peer_addr` without first dropping those borrows. Change to `&self` to match the underlying API and reduce friction at call sites.

```rust
pub(crate) fn peer_addr(&self) -> std::io::Result<Option<SocketAddr>> {
    match self {
        Self::Tcp(s) => s.peer_addr().map(Some),
        #[cfg(unix)]
        Self::Unix(_) => Ok(None),
    }
}
```

