The slowloris mitigation silently discards `set_read_timeout` errors with `let _ = ...`. If the syscall fails (EMFILE, EINVAL on a malformed timeout, etc.), the connection continues to be served with no read deadline, defeating the very protection the patch is meant to provide. At minimum log the failure with peer address and reason so an operator can detect a degraded server; ideally return the error or close the connection rather than handing it off.

```rust
let timeout_ms = crate::HTTP_READ_TIMEOUT_MS.load(std::sync::atomic::Ordering::Relaxed);
if let Err(e) = conn.set_read_timeout(Some(std::time::Duration::from_millis(timeout_ms))) {
    log::warn!("failed to set read timeout ({timeout_ms} ms) on {addr:?}: {e}");
}
```

