`accept` performs an atomic load plus a `set_read_timeout` syscall on every single TCP connection. While this is needed for slowloris protection, two optimizations would reduce hot-path cost: (1) only call `set_read_timeout` when the value has changed since the last accept (cache the last-loaded value), and (2) set `TCP_NODELAY` here as well so the small HTTP response writes aren't delayed by Nagle's algorithm.

```rust
let timeout_ms = crate::HTTP_READ_TIMEOUT_MS.load(std::sync::atomic::Ordering::Relaxed);
let _ = conn.set_read_timeout(Some(std::time::Duration::from_millis(timeout_ms)));
let _ = conn.set_nodelay(true);
```

