Unbounded header line growth enables a trivial memory-exhaustion DoS. `read_next_line` pushes every incoming byte into `buf` until it sees `\r\n`, with no maximum length. A peer that streams bytes without ever sending CRLF (or that sends CRLF only after, say, several GB) will force `Vec` to grow until the process is OOM-killed or the read times out. HTTP servers must cap header line size (typically 8–16 KiB). Replace the unbounded `Vec::new()` with a fixed-size stack buffer or add a `MAX_HEADER_LINE` check that returns `ErrorKind::InvalidData` when exceeded.

```rust
const MAX_HEADER_LINE: usize = 8 * 1024;
let mut buf = Vec::with_capacity(128);
loop {
    if buf.len() > MAX_HEADER_LINE {
        return Err(IoError::new(ErrorKind::InvalidData, "Header line too long"));
    }
    let byte = ...;
    buf.push(byte);
}
```

comments.suggestion_code:
fn read_next_line(&mut self) -> IoResult<AsciiString> {
    const MAX_HEADER_LINE: usize = 8 * 1024;
    let mut buf = Vec::with_capacity(128);
    let mut prev_byte_was_cr = false;

    loop {
        if buf.len() > MAX_HEADER_LINE {
            return Err(IoError::new(ErrorKind::InvalidData, "Header line too long"));
        }
        let byte = self.next_header_source.by_ref().bytes().next();

        let byte = match byte {
            Some(b) => b?,
            None => return Err(IoError::new(ErrorKind::ConnectionAborted, "Unexpected EOF")),
        };

        if byte == b'\n' && prev_byte_was_cr {
            buf.pop(); // removing the '\r'
            return AsciiString::from_ascii(buf)
                .map_err(|_| IoError::new(ErrorKind::InvalidInput, "Header is not in ASCII"));
        }

        prev_byte_was_cr = byte == b'\r';

        buf.push(byte);
    }
}

