`parse_header_value` advertises suitability for `Accept-*`/`TE` headers but does not validate the quality factor at all. `f32::from_str` happily accepts `NaN`, `±inf`, and any magnitude, while RFC 9110 §12.4.2 restricts q-values to the inclusive range `0.000`–`1.000` with at most three fractional digits. Downstream code that compares/sorts by q-value will misbehave (all NaN comparisons return false, so NaN headers will float to the top regardless of order), and negative/oversized weights will skew Accept-* negotiation. The parser should reject NaN/Inf, clamp or reject values outside `[0.0, 1.0]`, and ideally enforce the three-decimal-digit rule (or at least round to 3 digits) before storing the value.

```rust
let q_str = p.trim_start()[2..].trim();
if let Ok(val) = f32::from_str(q_str) {
    if val.is_finite() && (0.0..=1.0).contains(&val) {
        value = val;
        break;
    }
}
```

Consider also returning a `Result<Vec<(&str, f32)>, ParseError>` so callers can distinguish a malformed header from a missing q-parameter, instead of silently coercing everything to `1.0`.

