The unit test locks in non-conformant behavior: per RFC 9110 §12.4.2, q-values must lie in `[0.0, 1.0]`, but the test asserts `q=1.5` and `q=2.0` as valid output. If the parser is later tightened to reject out-of-range weights (which is the correct fix for the bug above), this test will break and discourage the fix. Replace the out-of-range inputs with spec-valid ones (e.g. `q=0.5`, `q=0.001`) and add negative tests covering `NaN`, `Infinity`, and values `>1.0` so future regressions are caught:

```rust
#[test]
#[allow(clippy::float_cmp)]
fn test_parse_header() {
    let result = super::parse_header_value("text/html, text/plain; q=0.5 , image/png ; q=0.25");
    assert_eq!(result.len(), 3);
    assert_eq!(result[0].0, "text/html");
    assert_eq!(result[0].1, 1.0);
    assert_eq!(result[1].0, "text/plain");
    assert_eq!(result[1].1, 0.5);
    assert_eq!(result[2].0, "image/png");
    assert_eq!(result[2].1, 0.25);
}

#[test]
#[allow(clippy::float_cmp)]
fn test_parse_header_rejects_invalid_q() {
    // Spec-violating q-values should not be passed through unchanged.
    for bad in ["text/html;q=1.5", "text/html;q=2.0", "text/html;q=-0.1", "text/html;q=NaN", "text/html;q=inf"] {
        let result = super::parse_header_value(bad);
        assert_eq!(result, vec![("text/html", 1.0)], "input: {bad}");
    }
}
```

